WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:05.120
Marvel has a lot of villains in its cinematic
universe, but which ones stand above the rest

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.920
when it comes to menacing Earth's Mightiest
Heroes?

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:13.339
To answer that increasingly complicated question,
we've watched every MCU movie and ranked all

00:00:13.339 --> 00:00:19.439
of their many bad guys, from Batroc and Surtur
to Whiplash and Thanos, and everyone in between.

00:00:19.439 --> 00:00:25.170
This list only focuses on tried-and-true villains,
so you won't find reformed heroes like Scarlet

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:28.650
Witch, Quicksilver, Nebula, or the Winter
Soldier.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.369
We're talking 100 percent baddies through
and through.

00:00:31.369 --> 00:00:33.790
So where does your favorite villain rank?

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:40.270
Here's our spoiler-filled take on every MCU
villain, ranked from worst to best.

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:41.640
Kurse

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:47.690
In The Dark World, Kurse leads a prison riot,
helps Malekith sack Asgard, and actually kills

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:49.690
Thor's mother, Frigga.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:54.640
He also leads the battle against Thor in the
film's climax, beating the Thunder God senseless

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:56.540
with his brute strength.

00:00:56.540 --> 00:01:01.390
Despite all that, Kurse is still little more
than a forgettable monster with no personality.

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:06.540
He's the ultimate mini-boss, and is in the
basement on this list's rankings.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:07.540
Laufey

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:12.780
Laufey is a powerful force in Thor's corner
of the Marvel Cinematic Universe, but we never

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.210
really see much of him in action.

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:20.409
King of the Frost Giants and biological father
to Loki, he has super-strength and ice powers,

00:01:20.409 --> 00:01:23.820
but he's still no match for the sneakiness
of his own son.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:28.490
Loki manipulates events to get Laufey to attack
Asgard in Thor, but just as Laufey is about

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:34.560
to kill Odin, Loki takes out Laufey instead,
simultaneously killing the Frost Giant king

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.990
and making himself look like a hero.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:39.300
Certainly an unceremonious end.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:40.840
Brandt and Savin

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:46.560
Aldrich Killian turned out to be the A-list
bad guy in Iron Man 3, with Brandt and Savin

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.390
shaping up as little more than B-list versions
with the same powers.

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:55.031
Both are given their super-heating and healing
powers by the Extremis virus, and they both

00:01:55.031 --> 00:01:59.240
attack Tony Stark throughout the film, but
neither is a match for Iron Man, even when

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:00.260
he doesn't have his suit.

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:01.260
Lame.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.729
The Scorpion

00:02:03.729 --> 00:02:07.840
He's a pretty big deal in the comics, but
in Spider-Man: Homecoming, not so much.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.910
The Scorpion's most notable actions are complete
failures, first when an arms deal is interrupted

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:17.909
by Spider-Man, and then later when he tries
to get the Sinister Six going in prison before

00:02:17.909 --> 00:02:19.730
being shut down by the Vulture.

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:23.510
Basically, he's just there to make Adrian
Toomes look cooler by comparison.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:27.799
And when you're not as cool as the Vulture,
it's safe to say you screwed up pretty bad

00:02:27.799 --> 00:02:29.540
somewhere along the way.

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:31.700
Batroc the Leaper

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:36.879
It's easy to forget, but yes, Batroc the Leaper
is part of the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:02:36.879 --> 00:02:41.340
We meet the French mercenary during the action
sequence that opens The Winter Soldier, during

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:44.379
the hijacking of the ship, the Lemurian Star.

00:02:44.379 --> 00:02:48.760
We get a breathless fight scene between the
two, with Cap accepting Batroc's challenge

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:51.389
for a hand to hand fight without his shield.

00:02:51.389 --> 00:02:54.909
Batroc holds his own, though Cap eventually
knocks him out.

00:02:54.909 --> 00:02:58.970
But hey, at least he's a little cooler than
this version.

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:00.450
The Shockers

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:04.139
Spider-Man: Homecoming gave us not just one,
but two Shockers.

00:03:04.139 --> 00:03:07.469
We first meet Jackson Brice wielding a Shocker
gauntlet.

00:03:07.469 --> 00:03:11.030
But after the Vulture kills him, the weapon's
passed to Herman Schultz.

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:16.859
Schultz looks to be our "main" Shocker in
the MCU, and he gets one epic fight against

00:03:16.859 --> 00:03:18.980
Spidey before being sidelined.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:23.309
Shocker jumps Peter as he leaves the school
dance attempting to follow the Vulture, and

00:03:23.309 --> 00:03:27.389
sends him flying through a school bus, and
he loses his web-shooters, too.

00:03:27.389 --> 00:03:29.590
So why does Shocker rank so low?

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:34.120
Because it wasn't even Spidey who took him
out, it was Pete's pal Ned, who grabs the

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:38.590
web-shooter and hits Shocker, distracting
him long enough for Spidey to web him to a

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:40.400
school bus.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:41.530
Shocking.

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:43.389
Baron Von Strucker

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:48.450
Strucker led the experiments on Loki's scepter
that created Quicksilver and the Scarlet Witch,

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:50.309
so he's definitely important.

00:03:50.309 --> 00:03:54.329
But all anyone really remembers about him
is his immediate surrender to the Avengers

00:03:54.329 --> 00:03:56.719
during the opening of Age of Ultron.

00:03:56.719 --> 00:03:58.360
"No surrender!"

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:00.010
"No surrender!"

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:03.510
"I am going to surrender."

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:07.189
In the end, Strucker was just another upper-level
Hydra goon.

00:04:07.189 --> 00:04:11.069
Cut off his head, and no one really noticed.

00:04:11.069 --> 00:04:12.620
The Destroyer

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:16.859
When the Destroyer shows up in Thor, it makes
full use of every moment.

00:04:16.859 --> 00:04:22.120
The Destroyer's giant, powerful arms and legs
can crush or stomp just about any opponent,

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:26.169
and if they don't kill you, its laser blasts
probably will.

00:04:26.169 --> 00:04:31.310
We saw the Destroyer kill a few Frost Giants,
then lay waste to a small town in New Mexico

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:33.780
when Loki sent it to Earth hunting Thor.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:38.510
Still, it was no match for Thor when the God
of Thunder regained his power and overcharged

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:41.710
the Destroyer, ending the attack.

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:43.240
Justin Hammer

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:48.289
Credit Sam Rockwell's unending charisma for
Iron Man 2's Justin Hammer not showing up

00:04:48.289 --> 00:04:53.440
at the very bottom of this list, or even further
down, if we could find a way to put him there.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:58.759
As far as credible villains are concerned,
Justin Hammer is an absolute joke, he runs

00:04:58.759 --> 00:05:04.389
an arms manufacturer that's a rival to Stark
Industries, except literally everything Hammer

00:05:04.389 --> 00:05:06.039
builds falls apart.

00:05:06.039 --> 00:05:11.419
The first version of the War Machine armor
is basically a bare-bones Iron Man suit outfitted

00:05:11.419 --> 00:05:15.990
with a ton of Hammer weapons, which works
about as well as duct taping accessories to

00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:17.060
a car.

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:19.729
To summarize: Sam Rockwell is great.

00:05:19.729 --> 00:05:22.800
Justin Hammer still kinda sucks, though.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:24.259
General Ross

00:05:24.259 --> 00:05:30.259
In The Incredible Hulk, General Thaddeus "Thunderbolt"
Ross spends the entire film chasing Banner,

00:05:30.259 --> 00:05:32.439
failing to kill him a few times along the
way.

00:05:32.439 --> 00:05:37.189
For much of the film, he's a relatively one-dimensional
"Military Bad Guy."

00:05:37.189 --> 00:05:41.319
Interestingly, however, Ross is one of the
few characters from The Incredible Hulk to

00:05:41.319 --> 00:05:43.750
resurface later in the MCU.

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:49.050
He became the U.S. Secretary of State in Captain
America: Civil War, pushing for the adoption

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:54.289
of the Sokovia Accords, and even made a cameo
in Avengers: Infinity War, setting him up

00:05:54.289 --> 00:05:58.080
for even badder bad guy stuff in films to
come.

00:05:58.080 --> 00:05:59.330
Abomination

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:04.699
In an effort to capture the Hulk after Banner's
alter ego punches him through a tree, former

00:06:04.699 --> 00:06:09.860
soldier Emil Blonsky hits himself with an
experimental version of the same gamma McGuffin

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:12.919
… except it doesn't turn him into another
Hulk.

00:06:12.919 --> 00:06:18.610
Instead, he becomes the Abomination, a monstrous,
one-note creature that looks like a rotting

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:19.610
troll.

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:20.610
Yawn.

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:24.879
He lived through the end of The Incredible
Hulk and is apparently in a jail cell somewhere,

00:06:24.879 --> 00:06:28.599
and the fact that Marvel hasn't really brought
him back since then tells you everything you

00:06:28.599 --> 00:06:30.360
need to know.

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:31.360
Malekith

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:37.569
Dark Elf Malekith had virtually no personality,
and basically existed as a freaky-looking

00:06:37.569 --> 00:06:43.069
dude trying to get an Infinity Stone, and
Thanos already does that way, way better.

00:06:43.069 --> 00:06:47.860
It's a shame, because on the surface, Malekith
is extremely formidable.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:53.229
He even stages a surprise assault on Asgard
itself, breaching the city's defenses, and

00:06:53.229 --> 00:06:54.760
later sets his sights on Earth.

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:59.229
He put up a heck of a fight against Thor in
the final act of The Dark World, though the

00:06:59.229 --> 00:07:01.810
God of Thunder still prevails.

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:03.389
Taserface

00:07:03.389 --> 00:07:08.830
As Rocket points out more than a few times,
Taserface's name is pretty dumb, but he still

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:13.000
has enough wits to lead a mutiny among Yondu's
crew of Ravagers.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:18.360
He's big, mean, ruthless, and conniving, and
brokers an uneasy alliance with Nebula to

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:21.479
take Yondu's ship in Guardians of the Galaxy
Vol. 2.

00:07:21.479 --> 00:07:28.560
He isn't all that bright, but hey, those other
four traits go a long way, for awhile, anyway.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:29.560
Whiplash

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:36.210
Mickey Rourke's Whiplash was one of many problems
in Iron Man 2, despite some admittedly awesome

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:38.000
action scenes along the way.

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:42.539
Whiplash never feels all that intimidating,
at least after that epic attack at the Monaco

00:07:42.539 --> 00:07:43.979
Grand Prix.

00:07:43.979 --> 00:07:49.770
He's motivated by a vague, kinda boring backstory,
and teams up with Justin Hammer, who, we've

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:52.330
already established, kinda sucks.

00:07:52.330 --> 00:07:54.379
And then there was that odd obsession with
his bird...

00:07:54.379 --> 00:07:55.599
Raza

00:07:55.599 --> 00:08:02.219
When Obadiah Stane decides to take out Tony
Stark while Stark's on an overseas tour in

00:08:02.219 --> 00:08:08.090
the first Iron Man film, he contacts the terrorist
organization the Ten Rings, led by Raza, to

00:08:08.090 --> 00:08:09.529
make it happen.

00:08:09.529 --> 00:08:14.639
Raza keeps Stark captive and proves an intimidating
villain, at least until Stark builds his first

00:08:14.639 --> 00:08:18.430
version of the Iron Man suit and makes short
work of Raza's soldiers.

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:23.320
Raza was savvy enough to survive, and he tracked
down stray bits of Stark's armor and tried

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:25.389
to cut another deal with Stane.

00:08:25.389 --> 00:08:26.449
The problem?

00:08:26.449 --> 00:08:32.400
Stane was more savvy than Raza, and used a
sonic taser to paralyze him and kill his men.

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:36.940
Despite his unceremonious ending, Raza is
still technically the first villain we met

00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:42.260
in the Marvel Cinematic Universe, and a throwback
to the simple days of the MCU.

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:43.970
Korath the Pursuer

00:08:43.970 --> 00:08:49.370
Korath has a small but memorable role in Guardians
of the Galaxy, which provided one of the most

00:08:49.370 --> 00:08:52.130
memorable moments from the movie's opening
scenes.

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:57.870
"Hey, there's another name you might know
be by…"

00:08:57.870 --> 00:08:58.870
"Star-Lord?"

00:08:58.870 --> 00:08:59.870
"Who?"

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:01.050
"I'm Star-Lord, man…"

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:05.190
Though Star-Lord gets the better of him in
the end, Korath still puts up a heck of a

00:09:05.190 --> 00:09:06.190
fight.

00:09:06.190 --> 00:09:11.070
He also manages to reacquire the Infinity
Stone along with Nebula, which sets up Ronan

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:14.910
the Accuser's near-world-ending attack on
Xandar.

00:09:14.910 --> 00:09:19.360
Without Korath, Ronan would've never had the
Infinity Stone to begin with, so he certainly

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:20.520
served a purpose.

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:25.660
He even holds his own against Drax the Destroyer
for awhile, at least until Drax rips out the

00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:28.320
cybernetic implant in his head, killing him.

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:30.940
Hey, at least he went out fighting.

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:32.460
Ulysses Klaue

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:38.570
Andy Serkis actually does an extremely entertaining
job with Ulysses Klaue in both Age of Ultron

00:09:38.570 --> 00:09:42.650
as well as Black Panther, which gives us a
believable version of the Klaw we know from

00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:47.220
the comics - a character who still has the
potential to come back after his "death" as

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:50.070
a dude made of hot-pink sound waves.

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:55.760
Hopefully the MCU will resurrect this classic
comics B-lister for future supervillainous

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:57.070
action.

00:09:57.070 --> 00:09:58.450
Sonny Burch

00:09:58.450 --> 00:10:03.270
Walton Goggins is a genuine delight every
time he shows up on screen, and his appearance

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:05.660
in Ant-Man and the Wasp is no exception.

00:10:05.660 --> 00:10:10.220
Sure, he doesn't do much compared to the likes
of Killmonger or Thanos, but he might be one

00:10:10.220 --> 00:10:13.250
of the MCU's most "comic booky" villains ever.

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:18.460
As a dealer in illicit technology, Sonny Burch
is a character that, like the Vulture before

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:23.940
him, operates on the fringes of a universe
filled with magic hammers and alien technology.

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:28.620
The difference is Burch is the kind of sleazy
mastermind who rolls around in a white-and-gold

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:33.820
SUV, conducts his illegal business in broad
daylight, and commands an army of nameless,

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:35.680
motorcycle-riding henchmen.

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:40.590
He's like a 1960s Batman villain, transplanted
directly into the MCU.

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:44.580
Burch is the perfect character to have around
to reinforce the idea that there's more to

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:49.090
this world than Shakespearean gods and magic
space rocks.

00:10:49.090 --> 00:10:50.370
The Tinkerer

00:10:50.370 --> 00:10:55.291
The Tinkerer is exactly the kind of character
that the MCU needed, for the same reason that

00:10:55.291 --> 00:10:59.240
he was exactly the kind of character that
the comics needed - someone who builds all

00:10:59.240 --> 00:11:02.210
the gadgets the bad guys use to fight the
good guys.

00:11:02.210 --> 00:11:06.760
He's certainly not the focus of Spider-Man:
Homecoming, but Phineas Mason is the kind

00:11:06.760 --> 00:11:11.410
of bad guy who makes the MCU feel just a bit
more real.

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:12.990
The Prowler

00:11:12.990 --> 00:11:17.850
Of all the impressive things about Spider-Man:
Homecoming, one of the most notable is just

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:20.340
how many characters the film pulled in from
the comics.

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:26.250
And the best minor villain appearance by far
is absolutely Aaron Davis, known in the comics

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:27.420
as the Prowler.

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:32.040
In the film, he's played by Donald Glover,
who acts as the thoroughly unimpressed straight

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:34.680
man to Peter Parker's "enhanced interrogation."

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:36.440
"What happened to your voice?"

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:37.660
"What do you mean what happened to my voice?"

00:11:37.660 --> 00:11:38.920
"I heard you by the bridge, I know what a
girl sounds like."

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:43.880
"I'm not a girl, I'm a boy, I mean I'm a man."

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:48.830
It's one of the best comedic scenes in the
entire MCU, but what really makes it special

00:11:48.830 --> 00:11:50.980
is Davis's mention of his nephew.

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:56.790
As comics readers know, Davis's nephew is
Miles Morales, who eventually takes the name

00:11:56.790 --> 00:11:58.000
of Spider-Man.

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:03.000
As far as villainy goes, Davis doesn't accomplish
much beyond a failed arms deal.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:06.810
Hinting at a future of the MCU that includes
Miles, though?

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:08.620
That's awesome.

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:10.050
Ayesha

00:12:10.050 --> 00:12:15.380
She might not have been the big bad in Guardians
of the Galaxy Vol. 2, but the golden goddess

00:12:15.380 --> 00:12:19.970
Ayesha was able to cause more than enough
trouble for Star-Lord and his team.

00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:24.170
The leader of the powerful Sovereign, she
commands a fleet of remote-controlled warships

00:12:24.170 --> 00:12:28.580
that come within one shot of taking out the
Milano following an extensive chase.

00:12:28.580 --> 00:12:33.730
Moreover, the post-credit scene revealed Ayesha
seems to be responsible for creating Adam

00:12:33.730 --> 00:12:38.330
Warlock, a major player in Marvel's cosmic
universe and someone we expect to meet in

00:12:38.330 --> 00:12:40.330
Guardians of the Galaxy Volume 3.

00:12:40.330 --> 00:12:45.350
If nothing else, that makes her more than
worthy of a slot on this list.

00:12:45.350 --> 00:12:46.870
The Chitauri

00:12:46.870 --> 00:12:51.330
These invading creeps basically served as
cannon fodder against Earth's Mightiest Heroes

00:12:51.330 --> 00:12:53.550
in the final hour of The Avengers.

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:59.100
Even still, they are a massive alien army,
led by those positively terrifying gigantic

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:01.770
dragon-like ships that tore through Manhattan.

00:13:01.770 --> 00:13:06.520
Their eventual humiliation notwithstanding,
the Chitauri were more than formidable enough

00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:11.020
to push the Avengers to the brink in their
first big screen team-up.

00:13:11.020 --> 00:13:12.740
Arnim Zola

00:13:12.740 --> 00:13:17.700
In Captain America: The First Avenger, Arnim
Zola's appearance is basically just an Easter

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:18.700
egg.

00:13:18.700 --> 00:13:23.290
In Winter Soldier, however, the setup of having
Zola around for the first movie pays off in

00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:24.510
a cool way.

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:28.730
He might not be a robot with a camera for
a head and his face on a giant TV screen built

00:13:28.730 --> 00:13:33.600
into his tummy like in the comics, but what
Winter Soldier's big reveal lacks in robot

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:38.670
bodies, it more than makes up for by making
him genuinely creepy.

00:13:38.670 --> 00:13:40.240
The Black Order

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:44.080
The Black Order are perfectly serviceable
henchmen for Infinity War.

00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:47.920
They manage to hit that sweet spot of being
threatening enough to present the heroes with

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:48.970
a challenge.

00:13:48.970 --> 00:13:53.120
But they also provide additional room for
the action so that it's not just 40 people

00:13:53.120 --> 00:13:55.550
trying to punch Thanos at the same time.

00:13:55.550 --> 00:14:00.000
Ultimately, they're not so threatening that
they overshadow the actual villain of the

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:01.000
piece.

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:05.140
Each of them is distinct and visually menacing,
especially Ebony Maw.

00:14:05.140 --> 00:14:09.270
Unfortunately, most of them don't actually
get to do much other than lose, so that's

00:14:09.270 --> 00:14:11.130
a bit of a drawback.

00:14:11.130 --> 00:14:12.830
Ronan the Accuser

00:14:12.830 --> 00:14:18.360
In Guardians of the Galaxy, Ronan was a religious
zealot who co-opted the Power Stone to continue

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:20.650
his crusade to wipe out Xandar.

00:14:20.650 --> 00:14:25.800
But even without one of those awesomely powerful
baubles, he's still a heck of a warrior.

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:30.180
After all, he laid waste to Drax the Destroyer
without even breaking a sweat.

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:34.620
He also had the guts to stick it to Thanos,
and actually walked away from the Mad Titan

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:36.380
without much consequence.

00:14:36.380 --> 00:14:41.480
The Guardians were no match for Ronan individually,
but luckily, Star-Lord was able to actually

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:46.210
control the Infinity Stone with the help of
his team, his half-Celestial powers, and those

00:14:46.210 --> 00:14:51.810
sweet dance moves, and they used its immense
power to blast the bad guy into oblivion.

00:14:51.810 --> 00:14:53.540
Yellowjacket

00:14:53.540 --> 00:14:59.030
Ant-Man's Darren Cross spent his entire career
trying to duplicate Hank Pym's shrinking formula.

00:14:59.030 --> 00:15:03.770
But when he did it and created the Yellowjacket
combat suit, he didn't realize his copycat

00:15:03.770 --> 00:15:08.510
formula was actually messing with his brain
chemistry, making him dangerously unstable.

00:15:08.510 --> 00:15:14.350
That led to a villain who was not only dangerously
unhinged, but got some really cool fight scenes,

00:15:14.350 --> 00:15:17.850
the best of which was on a child's play table.

00:15:17.850 --> 00:15:18.850
Ghost

00:15:18.850 --> 00:15:22.760
Ant-Man and the Wasp's super-powered villain
has a lot going for her.

00:15:22.760 --> 00:15:26.750
Visually, her flickering and phasing through
objects is some of the coolest-looking stuff

00:15:26.750 --> 00:15:28.680
we've seen in the MCU.

00:15:28.680 --> 00:15:32.880
It's even better in the fight scenes, the
shrinking and growing stuff is fun, but pitting

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:37.220
that stuff against a completely different
set of powers makes for some pretty compelling

00:15:37.220 --> 00:15:38.220
action.

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:42.480
Since she seemingly reforms at the end of
the film, we're obviously meant to like her.

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:47.770
She has a sympathetic backstory and motivation
as a victim of Hank Pym's egotistical past.

00:15:47.770 --> 00:15:52.500
At the same time, we're also supposed to like
Hank, so his part in Ghost's backstory still

00:15:52.500 --> 00:15:54.810
involves him ultimately being right.

00:15:54.810 --> 00:15:56.850
None of this is too surprising.

00:15:56.850 --> 00:16:01.360
Marvel is full of bad guys doing the wrong
thing for the right reasons, and vice versa,

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:05.720
but it does feel pretty clear that Ghost was
shoehorned into Ant-man &amp; the Wasp, rather

00:16:05.720 --> 00:16:07.400
than building her more organically.

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:12.440
Still, the effects are great, and Hannah John-Kamen's
performance captures Ava's understandable

00:16:12.440 --> 00:16:15.760
bitterness and desperation effectively.

00:16:15.760 --> 00:16:16.760
Surtur

00:16:16.760 --> 00:16:21.780
Diehard comics fans know Surtur is a major
villain in Thor's world.

00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:26.630
In Thor: Ragnarok, however, he's almost an
ancillary one, appearing only in the opening

00:16:26.630 --> 00:16:28.930
act and destructive finale.

00:16:28.930 --> 00:16:31.320
That's not to say he's not important to the
story.

00:16:31.320 --> 00:16:36.250
In Norse mythology, the word "Ragnarok" points
to the apocalyptic battle between gods that

00:16:36.250 --> 00:16:38.980
results in a world destroyed by fire.

00:16:38.980 --> 00:16:44.290
While Surtur is indeed the monster all Asgardians
fear, Thor and Loki summon him to invoke the

00:16:44.290 --> 00:16:46.220
Ragnarok prophecy.

00:16:46.220 --> 00:16:51.580
This defeats Hela, but at a price: his attack
decimates Asgard, crumbling the city in the

00:16:51.580 --> 00:16:53.890
sky to dust.

00:16:53.890 --> 00:16:54.890
The Mandarin

00:16:54.890 --> 00:17:01.010
Ben Kingsley's character in Iron Man 3 wasn't
actually the real Mandarin, and the "All Hail

00:17:01.010 --> 00:17:05.670
the King" one-shot short suggested the bona
fide bad guy could still be out there, waiting

00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:06.900
to make his move.

00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:11.310
But that "fake" Mandarin still gave us the
chills, at least until it was revealed that

00:17:11.310 --> 00:17:15.480
he's really just an out of work actor named
Trevor Slattery who can barely be trusted

00:17:15.480 --> 00:17:18.450
with a six-pack of beer, much less a gun.

00:17:18.450 --> 00:17:23.130
Still, though it loses some luster by the
end, the Mandarin presented in the front half

00:17:23.130 --> 00:17:27.330
of Iron Man 3 is one of the scariest baddies
in the MCU.

00:17:27.330 --> 00:17:29.530
It might've been a ruse, but it was effective.

00:17:29.530 --> 00:17:33.440
"And you'll never see me coming."

00:17:33.440 --> 00:17:34.860
Red Skull

00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:39.810
There was every chance it would come off as
silly, but Red Skull easily stands out as

00:17:39.810 --> 00:17:44.250
one of the most original and terrifying baddies
in the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:17:44.250 --> 00:17:49.410
In The First Avenger, he commands his own
HYDRA-branded Nazi division, complete with

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:54.880
Tesseract-powered weapons, and has his own
version of the Super Soldier Serum, making

00:17:54.880 --> 00:17:56.680
him Cap's physical equal.

00:17:56.680 --> 00:18:01.070
He vanished while trying to harness the power
of the Tesseract's Space Stone, but his surprise

00:18:01.070 --> 00:18:06.020
return as the guardian of the Soul Stone in
Infinity War only served to remind us how

00:18:06.020 --> 00:18:09.090
great he was so many years ago.

00:18:09.090 --> 00:18:10.570
Kaecilius

00:18:10.570 --> 00:18:14.960
He might've just been a disciple of Dormammu,
but Kaecilius was plenty intimidating his

00:18:14.960 --> 00:18:20.170
own right, a highly-trained sorcerer who went
rogue, left the Ancient One's order to try

00:18:20.170 --> 00:18:23.660
and take out the Masters of the Mystic Arts,
and nearly succeeded.

00:18:23.660 --> 00:18:29.550
In Doctor Strange, Kaecilius manages to destroy
two Sanctums, put the Ancient One's forces

00:18:29.550 --> 00:18:35.210
on the ropes, and shows his prowess with magic
in a few excellent fights against Doctor Strange.

00:18:35.210 --> 00:18:39.790
Luckily, a quick-thinking Strange managed
to turn the tables and win the day.

00:18:39.790 --> 00:18:44.800
Even if he was technically the B-list baddie
in Doctor Strange, he'll always be an A-lister

00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:46.640
to us.

00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:48.040
Crossbones

00:18:48.040 --> 00:18:53.970
Brock Rumlow started out as a member of Captain
America's strike team in Winter Soldier, eventually

00:18:53.970 --> 00:18:57.940
revealing himself as a HYDRA agent when the
attempted takeover began.

00:18:57.940 --> 00:19:02.570
He managed to survive, and resurfaced during
the opening fight in Civil War, where he went

00:19:02.570 --> 00:19:07.220
toe-to-toe with both Cap and Black Widow before
blowing himself up in the event that ended

00:19:07.220 --> 00:19:09.470
up driving the Avengers apart.

00:19:09.470 --> 00:19:12.830
For a villain, that's a pretty good way to
go out.

00:19:12.830 --> 00:19:14.200
Dormammu

00:19:14.200 --> 00:19:19.140
He's one of the biggest villains in Marvel
Comics, and the MCU actually kept the extremely

00:19:19.140 --> 00:19:23.770
powerful ruler of the Dark Dimension relatively
close to his comics counterpart.

00:19:23.770 --> 00:19:28.640
Doctor Strange's trip to the Dark Dimension
was practically a Steve Ditko panel brought

00:19:28.640 --> 00:19:29.750
to life.

00:19:29.750 --> 00:19:34.760
Dormammu is so powerful that Doctor Strange
had no chance of hurting him...and had to

00:19:34.760 --> 00:19:36.830
outsmart him to save the world.

00:19:36.830 --> 00:19:41.600
He trapped the villain in a time loop where
Dormammu killed him over and over, until he

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:43.540
was forced to bargain for his freedom.

00:19:43.540 --> 00:19:48.700
It makes for one of the most clever finales
in all of the MCU, and lands Dormammu high

00:19:48.700 --> 00:19:50.620
on our list of favorites.

00:19:50.620 --> 00:19:52.780
Alexander Pierce

00:19:52.780 --> 00:19:58.350
When Marvel signed Robert Redford for a superhero
movie, you knew it'd be an interesting role,

00:19:58.350 --> 00:20:00.970
and his turn as Alexander Pierce delivered.

00:20:00.970 --> 00:20:05.090
He's introduced as the well-meaning Secretary
of the World Security Council and old friend

00:20:05.090 --> 00:20:10.550
of Nick Fury…though it's eventually revealed
Pierce is actually a HYDRA agent leading their

00:20:10.550 --> 00:20:12.780
infiltration of S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:20:12.780 --> 00:20:17.030
Pierce shepherded the program designed to
create the networked helicarrier system everyone

00:20:17.030 --> 00:20:19.250
is fighting over in The Winter Soldier.

00:20:19.250 --> 00:20:23.280
He also planted bombs in the name tags of
the other Security Council members, which

00:20:23.280 --> 00:20:27.110
he uses to brutally murder them when they
challenge his takeover.

00:20:27.110 --> 00:20:32.410
He stayed evil to the end, too, uttering "hail
Hydra" with his dying breath after Nick Fury

00:20:32.410 --> 00:20:36.080
put two rounds into his chest.

00:20:36.080 --> 00:20:40.150
Now that's a villain.

00:20:40.150 --> 00:20:41.150
"Hail Hydra."

00:20:41.150 --> 00:20:42.820
Aldrich Killian

00:20:42.820 --> 00:20:47.770
Iron Man 3 is one of the most polarizing movies
in the Marvel Cinematic Universe, featuring

00:20:47.770 --> 00:20:53.560
an epic fakeout that reveals Guy Pearce's
Aldrich Killian is actually the villain behind

00:20:53.560 --> 00:20:58.220
a mysterious rash of bombings and terrorist
attacks sweeping the globe.

00:20:58.220 --> 00:20:59.400
His secret weapon?

00:20:59.400 --> 00:21:04.930
Extremis, a versatile technology that first
showed up in the Iron Man comics and is reimagined

00:21:04.930 --> 00:21:09.760
here as a way to super-heat one's body and
literally regrow limbs.

00:21:09.760 --> 00:21:13.920
Killian's particular skill-set makes for one
of the most technically ambitious fight scenes

00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:15.630
in the MCU.

00:21:15.630 --> 00:21:20.430
It's sometimes hard to believe any villain
can truly go toe-to-toe with Tony Stark, but

00:21:20.430 --> 00:21:22.790
Killian made a heck of a run.

00:21:22.790 --> 00:21:24.290
The Grandmaster

00:21:24.290 --> 00:21:29.360
When Thor is suddenly marooned on the junk
planet Sakaar, he comes in contact with the

00:21:29.360 --> 00:21:30.440
Grandmaster.

00:21:30.440 --> 00:21:34.880
In the comics, the Grandmaster is just an
immortal dude who loves playing games of any

00:21:34.880 --> 00:21:35.880
kind.

00:21:35.880 --> 00:21:40.660
But in the hands of Jeff Goldblum, the Grandmaster
becomes one of the weirdest and funniest characters

00:21:40.660 --> 00:21:42.160
in the MCU.

00:21:42.160 --> 00:21:45.910
Here's hoping we get to see a lot more of
him in the future - though maybe not as much

00:21:45.910 --> 00:21:47.210
as he wants us to.

00:21:47.210 --> 00:21:56.320
"On any other world I'd be like millions of
years old, but here on Sakaar…"

00:21:56.320 --> 00:21:57.900
Iron Monger

00:21:57.900 --> 00:22:04.559
You have to respect where it all began, and
Jeff Bridges' Obadiah Stane, a.k.a. Iron Monger,

00:22:04.559 --> 00:22:08.710
set the tone for the villains of the MCU in
the first Iron Man film.

00:22:08.710 --> 00:22:14.110
Bridges' Stane is positively slimy, betraying
Tony Stark and actually setting his entire

00:22:14.110 --> 00:22:19.380
hero's journey in action, so really, we can
thank Stane for kicking off the entire Marvel

00:22:19.380 --> 00:22:21.240
Cinematic Universe.

00:22:21.240 --> 00:22:26.230
When his assassination attempt on Stark in
the desert failed, Stane eventually donned

00:22:26.230 --> 00:22:30.580
the massive Iron Monger suit to try and finish
the job himself.

00:22:30.580 --> 00:22:35.830
A lot has changed since Iron Monger stalked
the skies, but his final fight with Iron Man

00:22:35.830 --> 00:22:41.791
had a brutal, messy feel that really let you
feel the hate Stane had for Stark, and it

00:22:41.791 --> 00:22:47.300
still resonates, even in a world with heavy
hitters like Thanos prowling around.

00:22:47.300 --> 00:22:48.650
Ultron

00:22:48.650 --> 00:22:54.110
James Spader brought absolute terror to the
metal menace that was Ultron, with a near-endless

00:22:54.110 --> 00:22:58.790
supply of robot baddies and a design that
had comics fans geeking out.

00:22:58.790 --> 00:23:03.910
He exemplifies how technology can be our greatest
scourge, though Ultron does it with a wit

00:23:03.910 --> 00:23:08.330
that only Spader can provide, verbally sparring
with the best of them.

00:23:08.330 --> 00:23:14.030
Like any great villain, Ultron also has a
real motivation for his dastardly deeds.

00:23:14.030 --> 00:23:18.570
Looking at the facts, Ultron's final determination
that humans and superheroes are what make

00:23:18.570 --> 00:23:23.490
the world such a dangerous place makes its
own twisted kind of sense.

00:23:23.490 --> 00:23:25.100
Helmut Zemo

00:23:25.100 --> 00:23:30.410
The most amazing thing about Helmut Zemo in
Civil War is he's just an average guy.

00:23:30.410 --> 00:23:31.490
No plans for world domination.

00:23:31.490 --> 00:23:36.800
Just a man who wants revenge for the death
of his family, and wise enough to realize

00:23:36.800 --> 00:23:41.040
the best way to take out the Avengers is to
set them against one another.

00:23:41.040 --> 00:23:46.890
Yes, his plan does require a few big leaps
of movie logic, but it was refreshing to see

00:23:46.890 --> 00:23:50.200
a villain like Zemo brought to life in the
MCU.

00:23:50.200 --> 00:23:54.210
He's a great reminder that it doesn't take
world-smashing superpowers to give Earth's

00:23:54.210 --> 00:23:57.600
Mightiest Heroes a run for their money.

00:23:57.600 --> 00:23:58.640
Ego

00:23:58.640 --> 00:24:02.860
Peter Quill's search for his father was a
key part of Guardians of the Galaxy Volume

00:24:02.860 --> 00:24:08.570
2, a journey that brought him face to face
with Kurt Russell's Ego, who's as bad a dad

00:24:08.570 --> 00:24:09.570
as they come.

00:24:09.570 --> 00:24:11.800
"Yes Drax, I've got a Snake Plissken."

00:24:11.800 --> 00:24:12.760
"Hah!

00:24:12.760 --> 00:24:13.700
Thank you!"

00:24:13.700 --> 00:24:17.820
Ego is one of the most powerful villains in
the Marvel Cinematic Universe, right up there

00:24:18.660 --> 00:24:21.040
alongside Thanos himself.

00:24:21.050 --> 00:24:26.730
Ego's essentially a god who wants to literally
become the universe by supplanting all other

00:24:26.730 --> 00:24:27.730
life.

00:24:27.730 --> 00:24:32.760
And Russell sells it so well that you really
understand where Ego's coming from, even if

00:24:32.760 --> 00:24:36.960
he's a psychopath who's killed hundreds of
his own children in his quest for power.

00:24:36.960 --> 00:24:39.809
Ego redefined the concept of daddy issues.

00:24:39.809 --> 00:24:42.900
"Of course I have issues!

00:24:42.900 --> 00:24:45.860
That's my freakin' father!"

00:24:46.460 --> 00:24:47.460
The Vulture

00:24:47.460 --> 00:24:53.170
In a universe of world-destroying monsters,
the Vulture is decidedly street-level in his

00:24:53.170 --> 00:24:55.580
approach, but that's exactly what makes him
work.

00:24:55.580 --> 00:24:59.390
The writers tweaked his backstory to make
Adrian Toomes the father of Peter Parker's

00:24:59.390 --> 00:25:04.350
teenage crush in Spider-Man: Homecoming, and
he takes the "girlfriend's angry dad" trope

00:25:04.350 --> 00:25:05.809
to a whole new level.

00:25:05.809 --> 00:25:10.250
The effects team also did an amazing job with
his armor and look, with the Vulture looking

00:25:10.250 --> 00:25:15.380
positively terrifying and inhuman when he's
stalking his prey.

00:25:15.380 --> 00:25:16.630
Hela

00:25:16.630 --> 00:25:21.490
We've never seen Thor face a villain like
Hela, the Goddess of Death in any of the other

00:25:21.490 --> 00:25:22.990
MCU films.

00:25:22.990 --> 00:25:27.990
Much like Loki before her, this Asgardian
villain is someone audiences will immediately

00:25:27.990 --> 00:25:29.200
love to hate.

00:25:29.200 --> 00:25:33.850
As soon as Odin passes away, Hela appears
to the brothers and presents her plan to take

00:25:33.850 --> 00:25:36.630
her rightful place on Asgard's throne.

00:25:36.630 --> 00:25:40.290
To prove her point, she easily crushes Thor's
hammer.

00:25:40.290 --> 00:25:45.490
The truth is eventually revealed that Hela
once acted as Odin's executioner, leading

00:25:45.490 --> 00:25:49.610
the Asgardian army to victory over all Nine
Realms.

00:25:49.610 --> 00:25:53.960
This secret history elevates Hela to epic
villain status.

00:25:53.960 --> 00:25:55.809
Erik Killmonger

00:25:55.809 --> 00:26:00.970
Erik Killmonger essentially has a superhero's
origin: his father is killed in front of him,

00:26:00.970 --> 00:26:06.160
his royal heritage is denied to him, and he
uses those tragedies to motivate a relentless

00:26:06.160 --> 00:26:10.820
dedication, training himself to the peak of
his abilities before seeking vengeance.

00:26:10.820 --> 00:26:16.090
That setup is a whole lot closer to Batman
than it is to, say, Crossbones or Ultron.

00:26:16.090 --> 00:26:21.309
When you add in the fact that Killmonger specifically
wants to address a continuing history of racism,

00:26:21.309 --> 00:26:24.040
it's hard not to admit that he makes some
pretty good points.

00:26:24.040 --> 00:26:28.291
Even if he's right, though, his goal is dominance
rather than leadership, which makes him a

00:26:28.291 --> 00:26:31.640
true ideological opponent for T'Challa.

00:26:31.640 --> 00:26:36.480
It's one of the things that makes their final
battle, in which they're both in nearly identical

00:26:36.480 --> 00:26:42.190
Black Panther costumes, so good: they're reflections
of each other, both committed to fight for

00:26:42.190 --> 00:26:46.250
their ideals without compromising who they
are.

00:26:46.250 --> 00:26:47.260
Loki

00:26:47.260 --> 00:26:51.780
While we originally said that villains turned
good guys weren't going to be on the list,

00:26:51.780 --> 00:26:56.240
we had to make an exception for the god of
mischief, especially since Loki was a baddie

00:26:56.240 --> 00:26:59.841
for multiple Marvel movies and caused so much
chaos throughout the MCU.

00:26:59.841 --> 00:27:00.841
There's a reason Marvel Studios chose to use
Loki as the main villain for The Avengers,

00:27:00.841 --> 00:27:01.841
he's one of the most compelling, calculated
and charismatic villains ever.

00:27:01.841 --> 00:27:05.190
Tom Hiddleston is so good at being bad that
Marvel opted to keep him around long after

00:27:05.190 --> 00:27:09.059
his bid for supremacy was foiled at the hands
of Earth's Mightiest Heroes.

00:27:09.060 --> 00:27:10.960
He's still no match for the Hulk…

00:27:12.700 --> 00:27:14.340
"Puny god…"

00:27:18.720 --> 00:27:20.280
...but who is?

00:27:20.290 --> 00:27:23.840
Oh, wait, we know who…

00:27:23.840 --> 00:27:24.840
Thanos

00:27:24.840 --> 00:27:27.570
Thanos is the villain of the Marvel Cinematic
Universe.

00:27:27.570 --> 00:27:31.740
There's been a lot of character work put into
the cinematic version of Thanos, which is

00:27:31.740 --> 00:27:35.960
especially impressive considering that most
of it is shown to the audience in Infinity

00:27:35.960 --> 00:27:41.679
War, a movie that's also juggling story arcs
for dozens of other characters at the same

00:27:41.679 --> 00:27:42.679
time.

00:27:42.679 --> 00:27:46.500
Through it all, he's shown to have the same
quality that all the greatest villains share:

00:27:46.500 --> 00:27:48.500
he thinks he's right.

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:53.930
He's the hero of his own story, the only one
who can step up and save the universe from

00:27:53.930 --> 00:27:58.750
itself, and is willing to sacrifice whatever
he needs to in pursuit of that goal.

00:27:58.750 --> 00:28:04.179
In the comics, Thanos has been referred to
as "the ultimate nihilist," but the MCU's

00:28:04.179 --> 00:28:06.120
version is the exact opposite.

00:28:06.120 --> 00:28:11.010
He believes very much in what he's doing,
which makes him even more compelling… and

00:28:11.010 --> 00:28:11.880
more dangerous.

